@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
' '   description:  A simple login collaboration: 3 roles exchanging sequence-numbered messages.
'   usecase:      Teach objects/roles, link lines, and sequence-numbered messages.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       web-auth
' ============================================================================
title Example Communication - Simple Login Collaboration

' A COMMUNICATION DIAGRAM shows OBJECTS (roles) connected by LINKS,
' with messages numbered in the ORDER they occur. Unlike a sequence
' diagram, the LAYOUT emphasizes WHO-TALKS-TO-WHOM (structure) rather
' than a strict time axis.

' PARTICIPANTS render as boxes. actor = stick figure; participant = box.
actor "User" as User
participant "LoginForm" as LoginForm
participant "AuthService" as AuthService

' LINKS are the association lines between objects. Use "--" for a solid link.
' Messages ride on links as arrows: "-->" for an asynchronous/call message.
' The label PREFIX "N:" is the sequence number that orders the messages.

User --> LoginForm : 1: enterCredentials()
LoginForm --> AuthService : 2: authenticate(user, pass)

' A NESTED sequence number (e.g. "2.1") means "a sub-message under 2".
' 2.1 happens DURING the handling of 2, before the caller at level 1 resumes.
AuthService --> AuthService : 2.1: hashPassword()

' Message 3 is the next TOP-LEVEL step — it happens after message 2 completes.
AuthService --> LoginForm : 3: returnToken()
LoginForm --> User : 4: showDashboard()

' TEACHING NOTES:
'   - Each link is a persistent association, NOT a one-off arrow.
'   - Numbers define the order; lower numbers happen first.
'   - 2.1 is a sub-call of 2 — useful for showing internal steps.
'   - A self-message (2.1) shows an object calling itself on its own link.
@enduml
